### Unit I — Four Kinds of Evidence

*Everything in this course rests on one of four families of data: **election
returns, the census, surveys, and administrative records.** Each is produced by
somebody, for a purpose, and each is silent about things the others can answer.
Three weeks here means every later lab arrives as a variation on something you
already know — Week 13 is the census with a threshold on it, Week 10 is a
survey with the error made visible.*

*The fourth family, administrative records, gets no week of its own. You meet it
in Week 5 (campaign finance filings) and Week 7 (congressional roll calls),
and you will recognise it when you do.*

**Week 1 — Election returns**

- **Tue, Aug 25** — Course introduction: the four kinds of evidence. Then
  straight into R with the 2024 electoral map.
  - *No reading — this is the first day.*
  - **Lab**: The 2024 electoral map. Getting started in R.
- **Thu, Aug 27** — Where returns come from, and why two sources disagree.
  - Alex Burness, "Election Data Is Vital to Voting Rights. So Why Is It So Hard
    to Track Down?" *Bolts*,
    <https://boltsmag.org/election-data-is-vital-to-voting-rights-but-hard-to-track-down/>
  - **Lab**: Sources, formats, and the pitfalls of official returns.
  - *The question the week answers*: **who won?** — which turns out to depend on
    who is counting, at what geography, and on which day.

**Week 2 — The census**

- **Tue, Sep 1** — What a census counts, and the rules built on top of it.
  - **Sides et al., Ch. 1** — the four standards this course evaluates
    everything against.
  - **Sides et al., Ch. 2**, and the **"The Electoral College" section of Ch. 9**
    (about six pages). Ch. 2 gives you districts, reapportionment and
    redistricting; that section of Ch. 9 gives you the Electoral College. Read
    together they are one story — how a population count turns into seats. We
    take the rest of Ch. 9 in Week 11.
  - Nate Cohn, "Republicans' Electoral College Edge, Once Seen as Ironclad, Looks
    to Be Fading," *New York Times* (Upshot),
    <https://www.nytimes.com/2024/09/25/upshot/trump-electoral-college-harris.html>
  - **Debate: Free choice vs. political equality.** Chapter 1 gives us the four
    standards and Table 1.1 says what each demands. Chapter 2 gives us the
    machinery — single-member districts, reapportionment, redistricting — that
    decides whether one person one vote means one person one *unit* of
    influence. Federalism says states choose their own rules; equality says a
    vote should weigh the same in Wyoming and California. Both cannot fully
    hold, and Thursday's lab puts a number on the gap.
- **Thu, Sep 3** — Getting census data, and what its numbers do not promise.
  - U.S. Census Bureau, *Understanding and Using American Community Survey
    Data*, Ch. 7: "Understanding Error and Determining Statistical
    Significance" — six pages, and the most useful six pages of methods writing
    in the course,
    <https://www.census.gov/content/dam/Census/library/publications/2020/acs/acs_general_handbook_2020_ch07.pdf>
  - **Lab**: Apportionment — how 435 seats get divided.
  - **In class, on your own screen**: pull one table from
    <https://data.census.gov> for a place you care about, and write down its
    margin of error. **No code.** The skill is knowing what to ask the site for
    and what the answer does not tell you.
  - *The question the week answers*: **how many seats does your state get, and
    how close was it?**

**Week 3 — Surveys**

- **Tue, Sep 8** — Who was asked, and how we know what anybody thinks.
  - Pew Research Center, "How Public Polling Has Changed in the 21st Century"
    (April 2023),
    <https://www.pewresearch.org/methods/2023/04/19/how-public-polling-has-changed-in-the-21st-century/>
  - Pew Research Center, *Public Opinion Polling Basics* — a free six-lesson
    course; do lessons 1–3,
    <https://www.pewresearch.org/course/public-opinion-polling-basics/>
  - **Debate: Deliberation.** A poll claims to tell you what the country
    thinks. Deliberation, as Chapter 1 defines it, requires that citizens have
    good information from many sources — and polls are one of the main things
    citizens are told *about themselves*. If response rates are under 1%, is a
    poll information or noise dressed as information? And if it is noise, what
    should a citizen do with the fact that everyone is reading it?
- **Thu, Sep 10** — **Lab**: What people say they did — the one survey we can
  check. The Census Bureau asks 60,000 households whether they voted, and the
  ballots are a matter of record. In 1972 the survey overstated the electorate
  by thirteen percent; by 2020 the gap was gone. Why it closed is the question.
  - **In class, on your own screen**: find a poll of a current race, report its
    toplines, and then find the crosstab that complicates it. Record the
    sample size, the mode, the population (adults, registered, likely voters),
    and the field dates. **No code.**
  - *The question the week answers*: **what do Americans think — and who,
    exactly, was asked?**

