### Unit II — The Campaign Machine

**Week 4 — you run this one yourself**

- **Tue, Sep 15** — **Class uncertain; assume no class.** I expect to be at the
  Eradicate Hate Global Summit. **Plan the week as though we do not meet**, and
  I will confirm on Canvas by **Fri, Sep 11**. If we do meet, we will spend the
  hour on Chapter 3 and the vote-targeting lab below — which you should have
  started either way, so a session would be a bonus and never a penalty.
- **Thu, Sep 17** — **No class.** Constitution Day Symposium. Go if you can —
  it is the most on-topic thing happening on this campus all term.

  **The week still happens, you just run it yourself.**

  - Read **Sides et al., Ch. 3** — the transformation of American campaigns.
  - Work through **Lab 4: A century of campaigns in data** on your own. It is
    the most self-contained lab of the term: every chunk runs as given, and the
    exercises are the usual "change one thing and tell me what it shows."
    **The board post still applies** — I cannot remind you at the end of class,
    because there is no class, so I am telling you here. This is the one week
    without a Friday deadline, since there is no Thursday session to follow:
    post any time before **Tue, Sep 22**.
  - **Second lab, also on your own: vote targeting in North Carolina.** Shorter
    and just as self-contained. It rebuilds the worked example Sides et al. give
    in Chapter 5 from the state's own returns — the book prints five counties,
    we do all one hundred, and we check its arithmetic while we are there.
    You are meeting the method two weeks before the chapter that explains it,
    which is the point: Chapter 3 is about campaigns becoming professional, and
    this is what professional turned out to mean.
  - **Debate: Deliberation, across two centuries.** Chapter 3 runs five campaign
    eras from 1788 to now, then closes with "The Case for Continuity" — its own
    argument that less has changed than it looks. Did the transformation of
    campaigns raise the volume of information while lowering its quality, or is
    that just nostalgia for an era whose deliberation excluded most people?
    Bring your answer to Sep 22; we will pick it up then.
  - **No data slide and no draw this week**, whether or not Tuesday happens.
    Nothing is owed and nobody can be penalised. Slides resume Thu, Sep 24.

**Week 5**

- **Tue, Sep 22** — Financing campaigns: who gives, who spends, and what it buys.
  - **Sides et al., Ch. 4**
  - "Donald Trump's Campaign-Finance Disclosures Are 'a Governance Nightmare',"
    *New York Times* (Opinion),
    <https://www.nytimes.com/2024/08/26/opinion/republican-donors-money-trump.html>
  - "Kamala Harris's Donors," *New York Times* (interactive) — skim the graphics,
    <https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2024/08/22/us/elections/kamala-harris-donors.html>
  - **Debate: Free speech vs. political equality.** This is the book's own
    "Debate over Campaign Finance Reform," and the sharpest collision in the
    course. Money is speech, and speech cannot be capped; but unequal money
    means unequal reach, and equality demands candidates be able to inform
    voters on comparable terms. *Buckley* chose. Would you? Note that the book
    also complicates the case for reform — take that seriously.
- **Thu, Sep 24** — **Lab**: FEC campaign finance data.
  - **Data journalism story: pitch your topic** (one paragraph, in class)

**Week 6**

- **Tue, Sep 29** — Modern campaign strategies: messaging, media, and targeting.
  - **Sides et al., Ch. 5**
  - **Debate: Deliberation vs. free choice.** Chapter 5 is about vote targets
    and profiling: campaigns decide in advance who is worth talking to. The
    voters who get contacted are informed and mobilised; the rest are left
    alone. Is targeting a rational allocation of scarce resources, or does it
    make deliberation something you receive only if a model scores you worth
    persuading? Tables 5.1–5.3 are the book's worked example.
- **Thu, Oct 1** — **Lab**: Campaign visits — where candidates go, and why.
  - *Note*: the campaign-visit counts in Ch. 9 (Table 9.3) cover the same ground
    as today's lab and disagree with our source. Working out why is one of the
    exercises.
  - You did Chapter 5's own worked example — vote targeting in North Carolina —
    back in Week 4. Go back to it now that you have read the chapter; the
    arithmetic reads differently once you know what the book claims for it.

**Week 7**

- **Tue, Oct 6** — Political parties and interest groups.
  - **Sides et al., Ch. 6–7**
  - G. Elliott Morris, "The Hidden Axis: The Left–Right Spectrum Has a
    Non-Ideology Problem,"
    <https://www.gelliottmorris.com/p/not-just-left-vs-right-most-voters>
  - Pascal Sabino, "Missourians Reject Measure That Threatened to Kill
    Citizen-Led Ballot Initiatives," *Bolts*, August 5, 2026 — Missouri voters
    rejected Amendment 4, which would have required a citizen initiative to win
    a majority in each of the state's eight congressional districts rather than
    statewide. It failed with about 80 percent against. Chapter 7 has a section
    on interest groups in ballot-initiative campaigns; this is that fight in its
    purest form, because the initiative *is* the mechanism by which citizens put
    something on the menu without asking a party or a legislature first,
    <https://boltsmag.org/missouri-rejects-restrictions-on-direct-democracy/>
  - **Debate: Free choice, and who sets the menu.** Chapters 6 and 7 both end by
    evaluating — "Evaluating Political Parties," "Evaluating Interest Groups."
    Parties narrow the choice set through nominations, and they also make choice
    legible to voters who know little else. Interest groups add information and
    resources, and represent members rather than citizens. Which does more for
    free choice, and which does more damage? Thursday's lab measures what the
    parties have become.
- **Thu, Oct 8** — **Lab**: DW-NOMINATE — measuring polarization in Congress.
  - *(No deliverable today — the data journalism draft moved to Oct 29 so you
    can write it with the campaign in front of you.)*

**Week 8 — FALL BREAK, no class (Oct 12–16)**

