### Unit III — Media and Voters

**Week 9**

- **Tue, Oct 20** — **TEST 1**, in class, on paper. **Sides et al., Ch. 1–5.**
  - Fall break is the week before, which is the point: you have it to study.
  - Read **Ch. 8** over the break as well — we take it up Thursday.
  - **Lab, in the time the test leaves: the midterm penalty, 1858–2026.** The
    president's party has lost House seats in **36 of the last 41 midterms —
    88%** — with a median loss of 28. One sentence forecasts the direction of a
    national election more reliably than any poll or model we meet this term.
    **You will write down a number before you leave**: how many seats the
    president's party gains or loses on Nov 3. Two weeks later, on **Nov 5**, we
    reopen this lab, enter the real result, and find out how you and the base
    rate both did. A prediction made after the fact is not a prediction, which
    is why we are doing this now and not then.

- **Thu, Oct 22** — Media effects, partisan media, and misinformation.
  - **Sides et al., Ch. 8**
  - Eady et al., "News Sharing on Social Media: Mapping the Ideology of News
    Media, Politicians, and the Mass Public," *Political Analysis*,
    <https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/S1047198724000196>
  - "How MSNBC's Leftward Tilt Delivers Ratings, and Complications," *New York
    Times*,
    <https://www.nytimes.com/2024/05/15/business/media/nbc-msnbc-trump-biden.html>
  - Stuart A. Thompson, "The MAGA Media Machine Is Fracturing," *New York Times*,
    July 31, 2026 — right-wing influencers feuding and shedding audience nine
    months before the midterms. Read it against Chapter 8's "Nationalization"
    section: the chapter's story is that media consolidated into a national
    market, and this one says the winning side of that market is coming apart.
    Both can be true. Work out how,
    <https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/31/us/politics/trump-iran-economy-media-critics.html>
  - **Podcast:** *Public Opinion Podcast* (AAPOR), "Misinformation in Public Opinion
    Research" — Amelia Burke-Garcia and Frank Graves on what happens when the
    thing you are trying to measure is itself contaminated,
    <https://aapor.org/media/public-opinion-pod/>
  - **Debate: Deliberation vs. free speech.** Chapter 8 closes by evaluating the
    news media's role, and the chapter's own material makes the case hard:
    coverage favours the horse race, algorithms curate what each voter sees, and
    news is a business before it is a civic institution. Every remedy — funding
    public media, regulating platforms, requiring disclosure — is a speech
    restriction. Which is worth it?
  - **Lab**: Media and misinformation data.

**Week 10**

- **Tue, Oct 27** — Voter participation: what drives turnout, and who benefits?
  - **Sides et al., Ch. 12**
  - "Inside the Rise of the Multiracial Right," *New York Times* (Opinion),
    <https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2025/07/24/opinion/minority-voters-trump-right.html>
  - **Debate: Political equality.** Chapter 12 asks the question directly — "Are
    Voters Representative of Nonvoters?" — and breaks participation down by
    income, age, race, gender, and race and gender together. If the electorate
    is not the country, is the problem the rules, the motivation, or the
    opportunity? The chapter separates those three; your answer should too,
    because they imply different fixes.
  - **Short lab, second half of class**: **GOTV cost-effectiveness.** You have
    $250,000 and nine weeks. Ch. 12's Table 12.1 — really Green and Gerber's
    synthesis of several hundred randomised field experiments — prices every
    mobilization tactic. Three of them turn out to do nothing at all.
- **Thu, Oct 29** — Voter choice; polling, sampling, and forecasting.
  - **Sides et al., Ch. 13**; Bailey, *Polling at a Crossroads*, pp. 3–22
  - Kselman & Niou, "A Taxonomy of Protest Voting," *Annual Review of Political
    Science*,
    <https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev-polisci-050517-120425>
  - Institute of Politics, Harvard Kennedy School, *Harvard Youth Poll*, 52nd
    edition — a live instrument to take apart on the day we do sampling. 2,018
    respondents aged 18–29, fielded March 26 – April 3, 2026. Read the toplines,
    then go find the methodology statement: how do you draw a national sample of
    that age group, what did they weight on, and what does the margin of error
    cover that the weighting does not? Note that you are the population being
    sampled here — check whether the results describe anyone you know,
    <https://iop.harvard.edu/youth-poll/52nd-edition-spring-2026>
  - *Optional, and fun*: "Can You Build an American Voter?" *The Economist*,
    <https://www.economist.com/interactive/us-2024-election/build-a-voter>
  - **Podcast:** *Public Opinion Podcast* (AAPOR), "Pre-Election Polling: The Challenges
    and The Importance of Understanding the 2024 Voter" — Don Levy. Pollsters
    talking to pollsters about why response rates collapsed, which is the
    problem today's lab simulates, <https://aapor.org/media/public-opinion-pod/>
  - **Lab**: Samples, questionnaires, and the ANES (or the Cooperative Election
    Study, <https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/X11EP6>)
  - **DUE: Data journalism DRAFT**, to Canvas before class. **This is your last
    word before the country votes** — write it as though it were finished,
    because next Thursday we read it against the result.

