### Unit IV — The 2026 Election

**Week 11**

- **Tue, Nov 3** — **DEMOCRACY DAY / ELECTION DAY, no class before 5p**
  - *Join us for CMU's Democracy Day, an opportunity to focus on our
    institutional commitment to civic service and democracy on Election Day.*
  - [**Register to vote**](https://www.cmu.edu/student-affairs/slice/civic-engagement/advocacy/voter/index.html)
- **Thu, Nov 5** — **The 2026 midterms: a live post-mortem.** Results against
  forecasts, using returns two days old.
  - **Sides et al., Ch. 9–11** (presidential, congressional, and state and local
    campaigns)
  - Jack Rametta, "2026 Senate Forecast Explorer" — read *before* Election Day,
    then judge it after, <https://jackrametta.com/senate-forecast/>
  - "The Age Issue: More of Congress Is 70-Plus Than Ever Before," *New York
    Times* (interactive),
    <https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2025/06/05/us/politics/congress-age.html>
  - Ferreira et al., "Inflation in 2022 Did Not Affect Congressional Voting, but
    Abortion Did," *PNAS*, <https://www.pnas.org/doi/abs/10.1073/pnas.2319512121>
  - "Trump and His Allies Mount a Pressure Campaign against US Elections ahead of
    the Midterms," *CNN*,
    <https://www.cnn.com/2025/08/02/politics/trump-elections-pressure-campaign>
  - **Podcast:** *Amicus* (Slate), "Trump Has a New 'Big Lie' for the Midterms"
    (Feb 7, 2026) — Dahlia Lithwick with Marc Elias, recorded nine months
    before the vote. **Listen before Election Day**, then judge it against what
    actually happened — same exercise we are doing with the forecast,
    <https://slate.com/podcasts/amicus/2026/02/midterm-elections-trump-bannon-ice-voter-suppression>
  - **Debate: Free choice in a nationalised election.** Chapter 10's headline
    claim is that congressional elections have been nationalised, weakening the
    incumbency advantage — and Chapter 11 asks what accountability survives in
    low-visibility state and local races. If a House vote is really a vote about
    the president, has the voter chosen a representative at all? Option B of
    today's lab answers this from the returns rather than the armchair.
  - **On off-cycle elections** (Ch. 11's most consequential section, and the one
    it treats most briefly). Daniel Nichanian, "To Boost Turnout, Ballot Measure
    Proposes Moving New York City Elections to Even Years," *Bolts*,
    September 5, 2025,
    <https://boltsmag.org/new-york-city-even-year-local-elections/>. The evidence
    is stark: in New York's 2021 off-year election, white turnout ran about five
    times Hispanic turnout, against roughly two-to-one in presidential cycles.
    Fort Worth's 2025 municipal turnout was under 10 percent.

    **Read it knowing the ending: the measure lost on November 4, 2025.** That
    makes it a better case, not a worse one. An off-year electorate was asked
    whether to abolish off-year elections and declined — and the electorate that
    made that decision was the unrepresentative one the reform was meant to fix.
    Bring that back to the Week 10 debate on political equality, and ask who
    should get to decide the rules that determine who shows up.
  - *Suggested, for the project*: Sarah F. Anzia, *Timing and Turnout: How
    Off-Cycle Elections Favor Organized Groups* (University of Chicago Press,
    2014) — the scholarly case that election timing is not an accident of
    administration but a durable advantage for whoever can turn out a small,
    committed electorate,
    <https://press.uchicago.edu/ucp/books/book/chicago/T/bo16956602.html>
  - *Before you read a single House result*: the map is not the one drawn after
    the 2020 Census. California is voting on a legislature-drawn map under
    Prop 50; Virginia is not, because its courts voided the equivalent measure
    in May. Several other states moved mid-decade too. Some of tonight's seat
    changes were decided before anyone voted — Week 15 takes this up properly.
  - **First, ten minutes: how did the prior do?** We reopen the midterm-penalty
    lab from **Oct 20**, enter the seat change the country just produced, and see
    where 2026 falls in a distribution of 41 midterms. **Bring the number you
    wrote down two weeks ago.** The question is not who was closest — it is how
    many of you beat simply reading the median off the table. A base rate is
    very hard to beat, which is the same lesson the polling lab taught from the
    other direction.
  - **Lab**: Election night data
  - *We reread the class's data journalism drafts, written before the vote, and
    ask which of them the result just embarrassed. You then get to revise —
    which is the whole reason the draft came first.*

