### Unit V — Representation: Who Counts, and Who Is Counted

**Week 12**

- **Tue, Nov 10** — **TEST 2**, in class, on paper. **Sides et al., Ch. 6–8 and 12–13.**
  - **Chapters 9–11 are not on this test.** You read them last week, during the
    election post-mortem; they are on Test 3, on Dec 3. Everything after this
    week is Unit V, which is not in Sides et al. at all and is not on any test.
  - Read the Census material below before Thursday.
- **Thu, Nov 12** — The Census: why we count, and who counts.
  - Scott, *Seeing Like a State*, Introduction
  - NCSL, *Redistricting Law 2020*, Ch. 1 (The Census)
  - U.S. Census Bureau, "America Counts: 250 Years,"
    <https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2026/06/america-counts-250-years.html>
  - Brennan Center, "Census Data Highlights a Changing Nation,"
    <https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/census-data-highlights-changing-nation>
  - **Debate: Political equality requires knowing who is there.** Equal weight
    per vote is arithmetic, and the arithmetic needs a denominator. Scott's
    argument is that states make populations legible in order to govern them,
    and that the categories are built by the counting rather than found by it.
    Against that: the categories track something real, and the Census is
    describing a country that is genuinely changing. The textbook never raises
    this; the standard it gave us in Chapter 1 does.
  - **DUE: peer reviews of the data journalism draft** — two each, worksheet on
    Canvas.
  - **Lab**: Census and ACS data (PL 94-171).
  - *Reference*: Census Academy, "Introduction to the Census Bureau Data API,"
    <https://www.census.gov/data/academy/courses/intro-to-the-census-bureau-data-api.html>

**Week 13**

- **Tue, Nov 17** — Classifying people; race, the VRA, and language access.
  - Nobles, *Shades of Citizenship*, Ch. 1 ("Race, Censuses, and Citizenship")
  - "Black Representation in Congress at Risk after Court Ruling," *NPR*,
    <https://www.npr.org/2026/04/30/nx-s1-5805050/supreme-court-voting-rights-congressional-black-caucus>
  - Don Moynihan, "Power, Democracy and Clarity,"
    <https://open.substack.com/pub/donmoynihan/p/power-democracy-and-clarity>
  - *Suggested*: "How the Black Vote Became a Monolith," *New York Times
    Magazine*, <https://www.nytimes.com/2020/09/16/magazine/black-vote.html>
  - **Podcast:** *Amicus* (Slate), "A Huge Shift is Underway at SCOTUS" (Jun 13, 2026) —
    Pamela S. Karlan on *Callais* and *Milligan*. This is the ruling behind the
    NPR piece above; Karlan argues it undoes the Second Reconstruction,
    <https://slate.com/podcasts/amicus/2026/06/justice-alito-leads-the-supreme-courts-sprint-to-change-the-face-of-american-democracy>
  - **Debate: Political equality vs. free choice.** The Voting Rights Act holds
    that formally equal rules can produce unequal outcomes, and that the remedy
    is to draw districts where a minority can elect a candidate of its choice.
    That is political equality asserting itself against a majority's free
    choice. After *Callais*, the Court disagrees. Which standard should give
    way, and who should decide — courts or legislatures?
- **Thu, Nov 19** — **Lab**: Section 203 coverage determinations.


**Week 14**

- **Tue, Nov 24** — **REMOTE — we meet on Zoom.** Link on Canvas. Surname
  analysis and BISG: inferring what the data doesn't say.

  **This is the only remote session of the term, and it is not a throwaway.**
  Nothing today is on a test — no part of Unit V is — and the lab is still the
  one that grades a method the Voting Rights Act runs on. All the code runs as
  given, so it works as well over Zoom as in the room: open the lab, follow
  along, and say something when a number surprises you.

  **This week runs backwards, because Thursday is Thanksgiving.** The lab is on
  Tuesday rather than Thursday, and today's session is where your attendance is
  recorded — submit what you have at the end, as you normally would on a
  Thursday. **No data slide and no draw this week**: both belong to a Thursday
  session that does not exist, so nothing is owed and nobody can be penalised.
  - Imai & Khanna, "Improving Ecological Inference by Predicting Individual
    Ethnicity from Voter Registration Records," *Political Analysis* 24 (2016)
  - **Lab, in two parts: the method, then the check.** We build the inference
    and then grade it, in one sitting, because the second half is worthless
    without the first and the first is far too flattering without the second.
    - *Surnames* — how a name becomes a probability, and what the 2010 Census
      surname list can and cannot carry.
    - *Grading the guess* — Houston County, Georgia records race on the voter
      registration form, so for once the inference can be checked against the
      truth. On 46,031 people it identifies Black voters correctly **40.3% of
      the time** — the method Voting Rights Act litigation runs on, measured
      against the group Section 2 exists to protect. Adding geography helps
      Black voters by 23.7 points and makes Asian and Hispanic voters *worse*.
  - **Debate: Political equality and inferred identity.** BISG works only to the
    extent that surname and geography predict race, and enforcement of the VRA
    increasingly depends on estimates like it. Equality here is measured with a
    model. If the model is wrong for you personally but right on average, has
    the standard been met?
- **Thu, Nov 26** — **THANKSGIVING, no class**

  **Board post, this week only: due Mon, Nov 30, 11:59 p.m.** — not Friday.
  There is no Thursday session to follow, and the Friday in question is the day
  after Thanksgiving. Post about Tuesday's lab any time before we meet again.

**Week 15**

- **Tue, Dec 1** — Racially polarized voting and ecological inference. Units,
  boundaries, and the modifiable areal unit problem.
  - King, *A Solution to the Ecological Inference Problem*, Ch. 1
  - Monmonier, *Drawing the Line*, Ch. 5 ("Boundary Litigation and the Map as
    Evidence")
  - **Lab: inferring the secret ballot.** Racially polarized voting is the
    factual question underneath every Section 2 case, and it has to be inferred,
    because nobody records how you voted. Houston County again — Georgia records
    both a voter's race and *which party's primary ballot they requested*, so
    for one election we know the answer the method is trying to guess. The
    fifty-year-old courtroom standard, ecological regression, fits the data with
    an **R² of 0.982** and concludes that **102% of Black voters** took a
    Democratic ballot. The truth is 92.7%. The method that cannot be wrong —
    arithmetic bounds on the precinct totals — gives an answer 30 points wide.
    Same county as last week, same registration file, same move: find the rare
    case where an unfalsifiable method can be graded.
  - **Debate: Political equality, and what counts as proof.** Racially polarized
    voting is the empirical question underneath the VRA, and the one courts
    actually rule on. Ecological inference infers individual behaviour from
    aggregate data — the thing every statistics course warns against. Should a
    constitutional standard rest on an inference of this kind? What is the
    alternative?
- **Thu, Dec 3** — Redistricting and gerrymandering; census privacy and the
  TopDown algorithm. Course wrap.
  - NCSL, *Redistricting Law 2020*, Ch. 4 (Redistricting Principles and Criteria)
  - Duchin & Walch, eds., *Political Geometry*, Ch. 16 (Redistricting Algorithms)
  - "Gerrymandered Districts Aren't Always Ugly," *New York Times* (Opinion,
    interactive) — a direct challenge to the eyeball test,
    <https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/01/11/opinion/redistricting-gerrymandering-reform.html>
  - "Is Massachusetts a Gerrymandered State?" *New York Times*
  - **Two ballot measures, two outcomes, one year.** Read these together; they
    are the same question asked twice and answered differently.
    - **California, Proposition 50** (passed Nov 4, 2025). Suspends the
      independent commission and uses a legislature-drawn congressional map
      from 2026 through 2030, after which the commission resumes. Projected to
      net Democrats roughly three to five seats, and it survived a federal
      racial-gerrymandering challenge in January 2026. Legislative Analyst's
      neutral summary:
      <https://lao.ca.gov/BallotAnalysis/Proposition?number=50&year=2025>;
      what it did to the map:
      <https://www.kqed.org/news/12064834/how-prop-50-just-rewrote-californias-2026-congressional-map>
    - **Virginia** (referendum Apr 21, 2026). A near-identical move — return
      map-drawing to the legislature — approved **50.7 to 49.3**, and then
      **voided by the Supreme Court of Virginia on May 8, 2026**, 4–3. The
      ruling turned not on gerrymandering but on procedure: whether 45 days of
      early voting count as part of the "intervening election" the state
      constitution requires between the two legislative passages of an
      amendment.
      <https://cardinalnews.org/2026/05/08/supreme-court-of-virginia-voids-redistricting-election-as-unconstitutional/>;
      Brennan Center on what it means:
      <https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/va-redistricting-referendum-shows-peril-scotus-gerrymandering-rulings>

    Three states have now put the districting rules themselves on a ballot —
    these two and Missouri in Week 7. One passed and stuck, one passed and was
    undone on a technicality about early voting, one failed at the polls. If
    the rules can be changed mid-decade by whoever currently holds the pen,
    what is a districting commission actually for?
  - **Podcast:** *Amicus* (Slate), "The Term the Court Stopped Pretending" (Jul 11, 2026)
    — Sherrilyn Ifill, Nikolas Bowie and Stephen Vladeck on the term that
    rewrote voting rights. A fitting last listen: the law changed underneath
    everything we measured this semester,
    <https://slate.com/podcasts/amicus/2026/07/supreme-court-term-recap-birthright-citizenship-voting-rights>
  - **Debate: All four standards at once.** The closing question, and the one
    every week has been circling: compared to what? Districts have to be drawn
    only because we elect one person per district. Proportional representation
    has no districts to gerrymander; ranked-choice keeps them but changes what
    winning requires. Score each system against all four standards — free
    choice, political equality, deliberation, free speech — and say honestly
    which one you are willing to lose. Ask what each system fixes, what it
    merely relocates, and what it costs.
  - **TEST 3**, in class, on paper — **Sides et al., Ch. 9–11**, the chapters
    you read on election week. Nothing else is on it: not today's redistricting
    material, not Unit V, not the labs.
  - **No lab today — but one last set of numbers, worked at the front of the
    room in whatever time the test leaves.** The *Times* piece above argues that
    you cannot identify a gerrymander by looking at it. That is correct, and it
    raises the obvious question: then what *can* you look at? The answer is the
    **distribution** of district partisanship, not the shape of any district in
    it. Across all 434 districts the mean Democratic share is **49.9%** and
    Democrats hold **205 seats (47.2%)** — close to fair in the aggregate, and
    the aggregate is hiding everything. Only **91 districts, 21%, sit within
    five points of even**: there is a hole where the competitive seats should
    be, and that hole is what a decade of map-drawing produced. By state, the
    efficiency gap runs from **North Carolina at −25.6 and Wisconsin at −25.4**
    to **Massachusetts at +22.4 and Illinois at +19.0**, with **Pennsylvania at
    −1.7**, nearly unbiased.
  - **And then the number that breaks the number.** Massachusetts scores as
    lopsided as North Carolina, and **Massachusetts is not gerrymandered.** It
    is 63.8% Democratic and elects Democrats in nine seats out of nine, because
    there is no way to carve a Republican seat out of Massachusetts without
    setting out to do it. The efficiency gap cannot tell a drawn advantage from
    a geographic one — it just reports that the outcome is skewed. So the
    measure that was going to rescue us from the eyeball test has its own blind
    spot, and it is a large one.
  - **Why this is the last thing you see.** Every measure above is a number
    standing in for a value — the same trade you watched on Tuesday, when
    ecological regression was precise and impossible and the honest bounds were
    thirty points wide. The efficiency gap is one answer to *compared to what?*,
    it is contested, and the Supreme Court declined to adopt it. You are not
    being asked to accept it. You are being asked to notice that the argument
    now happens in the units, and that whoever picks the measure has already
    won half the fight.
  - **If you want to draw one yourself**, Dave's Redistricting is free and is
    what the professionals actually use — <https://davesredistricting.org>. Not
    assigned, not graded; it is the thing to open the next time you hear that a
    map is unfair and want to find out for yourself.

***

**Finals period — there is no exam.** All three tests were held during the
term, the last of them on Dec 3; nothing is scheduled in the exam block.

- **DUE Fri, Dec 11** — **Data journalism story, final version**, with your
  note on what you changed. You drafted it before the election and are revising
  it after; say what the result did to your argument. This is the last thing you
  owe me.

